<section xml:id="laplace04" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Convolution</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li><p>To understand that if <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions, then we can define the <term>convolution product</term> of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> to be
                <md>(f*g)(t) = \int_0^t f(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d\tau = \int_0^t f(\tau) g(t - \tau) \, d\tau.</md></p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that the convolution product has many properties similar to those of ordinary multiplication.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that if <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions and  <m>{\mathcal L}(f)(s) = F(s)</m> and <m>{\mathcal L}(g)(s) = G(s)</m> for <m>s \geq a \gt 0</m>, then
                <md>F(s) G(s) = {\mathcal L}(f*g)(s)</md>
            for <m>s \gt a</m>.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that it is possible to write a solution for the initial value problem
                <md>
                    <mrow>ay'' + by' + cy &amp; = g(t)</mrow>
                    <mrow>y(0) &amp; = y_0</mrow>
                    <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = y_1.</mrow>
                </md>
            using the <term>Convolution Theorem</term>.</p></li>

        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>When solving an initial value problem using Laplace transforms, we employed the strategy of converting the differential equation to an algebraic equation. Once the the algebraic equation is solved, we can recover the solution to the initial value problem using the inverse Laplace transform. While our strategy is straight forward, finding an inverse Laplace tranform can be a hindrance. Often finding the inverse transform involves decomposing a complicated product into the sum for partial fractions. Fortunately, there is a product rule for inverse Laplace transforms. This product rule will allow us to quickly compute solutions of a harmonic oscillator with different forcing functions.</p>
    </introduction>
    
    <subsection xml:id="laplace04-subsection-convolution">
        <title>Convolution</title>

        <p>If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions, then we define the <term>convolution product</term> of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> to be
            <md>(f*g)(t) = \int_0^t f(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d\tau = \int_0^t f(\tau) g(t - \tau) \, d\tau.</md>
        The convolution product has many properties similar to those of ordinary multiplication.
            <ul>

                <li>Commutivity: <m>f*g = g*f</m>.</li>

                <li>Distribution: <m>f*(g + h) = f*g + f*h</m>.</li>

                <li>Associativity:  <m>f*(g*h) = (f*g)*h</m>.</li>

                <li><m>0 *f = f*0 = 0</m>.</li>

            </ul>All of these properties can be proven using the definition of convolution and the properties of integration.</p>

        <notation>
            <usage><m>f*g</m></usage>
            <description>convolution product</description>
        </notation>
        
        <p>There is, however, no multiplicative identity. In other words, <m>f*1 \neq f</m>. For example, suppose that <m>f(t) = \cos t</m>.  Then
            <md>(f*1)(t) = \int_0^t \cos(t - \tau) \cdot 1 \, d\tau = \sin t.</md>
        Also, it may not be the case that <m>f*f</m> is a nonnegative function.</p>

        <p>A key property of the Laplace transform is how convolution products behave.</p>

        <theorem xml:id="laplace04-theorem-convolution">
            <title>Convolution Theorem</title><idx>Convolution Theorem</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions, and suppose that <m>{\mathcal L}(f)(s) = F(s)</m> and <m>{\mathcal L}(g)(s) = G(s)</m> for <m>s \geq a \gt 0</m>. Then
                    <md>F(s) G(s) = {\mathcal L}(f*g)(s)</md>
                for <m>s \gt a</m>.</p>
            </statement>
        </theorem> 

        <proof>
            <p>If
                <md>
                    <mrow>F(s) &amp; = \int_0^\infty e^{-s\xi} f(\xi) \, d\xi</mrow>
                    <mrow>G(s) &amp; = \int_0^\infty e^{-s\tau} g(\tau) \, d\tau,</mrow>
                </md>
            then
                <md>
                    <mrow>F(s) G(s) &amp; = \int_0^\infty e^{-s\xi} f(\xi) \, d\xi \int_0^\infty e^{-s\tau} g(\tau) \, d\tau</mrow>
                    <mrow>&amp; = \int_0^\infty g(\tau) \left( \int_\tau^\infty e^{-st} f(t - \tau) \, dt \right) \, d\tau,</mrow>
                </md>
            where <m>\xi = t - \tau</m> is the change of variable. Reversing the order of integration, we have
                <md>\int_0^\infty g(\tau) \left( \int_\tau^\infty e^{-st} f(t - \tau) \, dt \right) \, d\tau = \int_0^\infty  e^{-st} \left( \int_0^t f(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d\tau \right) \, dt.</md>
            However, this last expression is just the Laplace transform of <m>f*g</m>.</p>
        </proof>
    </subsection>
    
    <subsection xml:id="laplace04-subsection-applying-convolution">
        <title>Applying the Convolution Theorem</title>

        <p>The Convolution Theorem (<xref ref="laplace04-theorem-convolution" />) can be very useful for finding the inverse transforms of products, since
            <md>F(s) G(s) = {\mathcal L}(f*g)(s).</md>
        For example, suppose that we wish to find the inverse transform of 
            <md>\frac{1}{s^2(s + 1)} = \frac{1}{s^2} \cdot \frac{1}{s + 1}.</md>
        Since <m>{\mathcal L}[t] = 1/s^2</m> and <m>{\mathcal L}[e^{-t}] = 1/(s + 1)</m>, we know that
            <md>
                <mrow>{\mathcal L}^{-1} \left[ \frac{1}{s^2(s + 1)}\right] &amp; = {\mathcal L}^{-1} \left[ \frac{1}{s^2} \right] * {\mathcal L}^{-1} \left[ \frac{1}{s + 1} \right]</mrow>
                <mrow>&amp; = \int_0^t (t - u) e^{-u}\, du</mrow>
                <mrow>&amp; = t + e^{-t} - 1.</mrow>
            </md>
        Of course, we could have used partial fractions to decompose <m>1/(s^2 (s + 1))</m>.</p>




        <example>
            <p>Instead of using partial fractions, let us use the Convolution Theorem (<xref ref="laplace04-theorem-convolution" />) to calculate the inverse Laplace transform of
                <md>H(s)= \frac{a}{s^2(s^2 + a^2)} = \frac{1}{s^2} \cdot \frac{a}{s^2 + a^2}.</md>
            The inverse Laplace transform of <m>1/s^2</m> is <m>t</m>, and the inverse Laplace transform of <m>a/(s^2 + a^2)</m>. is <m>\sin at</m>. Using the Convolution Theorem, the inverse Laplace transform of <m>H(s)</m> is
                <md>h(t) = \int_0^t (t - \tau) \sin a \tau \, d \tau = \frac{at - \sin at}{a^2}.</md></p>
        </example>

        <p>We can also use the Convolution Theorem to solve initial value problems.</p>

        <example xml:id="laplace04-example-convolution">
            <p>Consider the initial value problem
                <md>
                    <mrow>y'' + 4y &amp; = g(t)</mrow>
                    <mrow>y(0) &amp; = 3</mrow>
                    <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = -1.</mrow>
                </md>
            Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation and applying the initial conditions, we obtain
                <md>s^2 Y(s) - 3s + 1 + 4Y(s) = G(s),</md>
            where <m>G(s)</m> is the Laplace transform of <m>g(t)</m>. Solving for <m>Y(s)</m>, we have
                <md>Y(s) = \frac{3s-1}{s^2 + 4} + \frac{G(s)}{s^2 + 4} = 3\frac{s}{s^2 + 4} - \frac{1}{2} \frac{2}{s^2 + 4} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{2}{s^2 + 4} G(s).</md>
            The last term corresponds to the forcing term of our differential equation. Taking the inverse Laplace transform of both sides and applying the Convolution Theorem, we get
                <md>y = 3 \cos 2t - \frac{2}{2} \sin 2t + \frac{1}{2} \int_0^t \sin 2(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d \tau.</md></p>
        </example>

        <p>Notice that we did not specify a particular forcing function in <xref ref="laplace04-example-convolution" />.  In fact, it is possible to write a solution for the initial value problem
            <md>
                <mrow>ay'' + by' + cy &amp; = g(t)</mrow>
                <mrow>y(0) &amp; = y_0</mrow>
                <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = y_1</mrow>
            </md>
        using the Convolution Theorem without knowing what the actual forcing function <m>g(t)</m>. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation and using the initial conditions, we have
            <md>(as^2 + bs + c)Y(s) - (as + b)y_0 - ay_1 = G(s)</md>
        or
            <md>Y(s) = \Phi(s) + \Psi(s),</md>
        where
            <md>
                <mrow>\Phi(s) &amp; = \frac{(as + b)y_0 + ay_1 }{as^2 + bs + c}</mrow>
                <mrow>\Psi(s) &amp; = \frac{G(s)}{as^2 + bs + c}.</mrow>
            </md>
        Therefore,
            <md>y = \phi(t) + \psi(t),</md>
        where <m>\phi(t) = {\mathcal L}^{-1}( \Phi(s))</m> and <m> \psi(t) = {\mathcal L}^{-1}( \Psi(s))</m>. Observe that <m>\phi(t)</m> is the solution to the initial value problem
            <md>
                <mrow>ay'' + by' + cy &amp; = 0</mrow>
                <mrow>y(0) &amp; = y_0</mrow>
                <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = y_1.</mrow>
            </md>
        Once we have values for <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>, the function <m>\phi(t)</m> is easy to find. To find <m>\psi(t)</m>, we first write 
            <md>\Psi(s) = \frac{1}{as^2 + bs + c} G(s) = H(s) G(s),</md>
        where <m>H(s) = 1/(as^2 + bs + c)</m>.  If <m>h(t)</m> is the inverse Laplace transform of <m>H(s)</m>, then
            <md>\psi(t)  = {\mathcal L}^{-1}(H(s)G(s)) = \int_0^t h(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d\tau.</md></p>

        <p>If we consider the case where <m>G(s) = 1</m>, then <m>g(t) = \delta(t)</m> and <m>\psi(s) = H(s)</m>. This means that <m>h(t)</m> is a solution to the initial value problem
            <md>
                <mrow>ay'' + by' + cy &amp; = \delta(t)</mrow>
                <mrow>y(0) &amp; = y_0</mrow>
                <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = y_1.</mrow>
            </md>
        For this reason, <m>h(t)</m> is sometimes called the <term>impulse response</term><idx>impulse response</idx> of the system.</p>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="laplace04-subsection-important-lessons">
        <title>Important Lessons</title>

        <p><ul>

            <li>If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions, then we define the <term>convolution product</term> of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> to be
                <md>(f*g)(t) = \int_0^t f(t - \tau) g(\tau) \, d\tau = \int_0^t f(\tau) g(t - \tau) \, d\tau.</md></li>

            <li><p>The convolution product has many properties similar to those of ordinary multiplication.<ul>

                <li>Commutivity: <m>f*g = g*f</m></li>

                <li>Distribution: <m>f*(g + h) = f*g + f*h</m></li>

                <li>Associativity:  <m>f*(g*h) = (f*g)*h</m></li>

                <li><m>0 *f = f*0 = 0</m></li>

            </ul>There is, however, no multiplicative identity. In other words, <m>f*1 \neq f</m>.  Also, it may not be the case that <m>f*f</m> is a nonnegative function.</p></li>

            <li>Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be two piecewise continuous exponentially bounded functions, and suppose that <m>{\mathcal L}(f)(s) = F(s)</m> and <m>{\mathcal L}(g)(s) = G(s)</m> for <m>s \geq a \gt 0</m>. Then
                <md>F(s) G(s) = {\mathcal L}(f*g)(s)</md>
            for <m>s \gt a</m>.</li>

            <li>It is possible to write a solution for the initial value problem
                <md>
                    <mrow>ay'' + by' + cy &amp; = g(t)</mrow>
                    <mrow>y(0) &amp; = y_0</mrow>
                    <mrow>y'(0) &amp; = y_1.</mrow>
                </md>
            using the Convolution Theorem.</li>
        
        </ul></p>
        
    </subsection>

    <reading-questions xml:id="reading-questions-laplace04">

        <exercise xml:id="reading-questions-laplace04-1" label="rq-laplace04-convolution">
            <statement>
                <p>Explain how the convolution product of two functions is useful when solving an initial value problem.</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </exercise>

    </reading-questions>

    <exercises xml:id="laplace04-exercises" filenamebase="laplace04">
        <title>Exercises</title>

         <exercisegroup xml:id="laplace04-exercises-convolution">
            <title>Convolution of Two Functions</title>
            <introduction><p>Calculate the convolution product of <m>f(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m> in <xref ref="laplace04-exercises-convolution"/> using the definition of the convolution of two functions.</p></introduction>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = 2</m> and <m>g(t) = t</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                <p>
                    By definition,
                    <md>
                    (f*g)(t)=\int_0^t 2(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                    </md>
                    Evaluating,
                    <md>
                    (f*g)(t)=2\int_0^t (t-\tau)\,d\tau
                    =t^2.
                    </md>
                </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = t</m> and <m>g(t) = t</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                      <p>By definition,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) &amp;= \int_0^t f(\tau)\, g(t - \tau)\, d\tau
                                           = \int_0^t \tau (t - \tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
                          <mrow>&amp;= \int_0^t (t\tau - \tau^2)\, d\tau
                                 = \left[ \frac{t\tau^2}{2} - \frac{\tau^3}{3} \right]_0^t
                                 = \frac{t^3}{2} - \frac{t^3}{3}
                                 = \frac{t^3}{6}.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Therefore <m>(f * g)(t) = \dfrac{t^3}{6}</m>.
                      </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>            
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = t</m> and <m>g(t) = e^{3t}</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                      <p>By definition,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) &amp;= \int_0^t \tau\, e^{3(t - \tau)}\, d\tau
                                           = e^{3t}\int_0^t \tau\, e^{-3\tau}\, d\tau.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Integrating by parts with <m>u = \tau</m> and
                      <m>dv = e^{-3\tau}\,d\tau</m>,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>\int_0^t \tau\, e^{-3\tau}\, d\tau
                              &amp;= \left[ -\frac{\tau}{3}e^{-3\tau}
                                 - \frac{1}{9}e^{-3\tau} \right]_0^t
                               = -\frac{t}{3}e^{-3t} - \frac{1}{9}e^{-3t} + \frac{1}{9}.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Multiplying by <m>e^{3t}</m> gives
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) = -\frac{t}{3} - \frac{1}{9} + \frac{e^{3t}}{9}
                                           = \frac{e^{3t} - 3t - 1}{9}.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>            
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = t</m> and <m>g(t) = \sin t</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                    <p>By definition,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) = \int_0^t \tau \sin(t - \tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Substituting <m>s = t - \tau</m>, so <m>\tau = t - s</m> and
                      <m>d\tau = -ds</m>,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) &amp;= \int_0^t (t - s)\sin s\, ds</mrow>
                                           <mrow> \amp = t\int_0^t \sin s\, ds - \int_0^t s\sin s\, ds</mrow>
                          <mrow>&amp;= t(1 - \cos t) - \bigl[\sin s - s\cos s\bigr]_0^t</mrow>
                                 <mrow> \amp = t - t\cos t - \sin t + t\cos t.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Therefore
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) = t - \sin t.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>            
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = e^{2t}</m> and <m>g(t) = e^{3t}</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>By definition,
                    <md>
                      <mrow>(f * g)(t) &amp;= \int_0^t e^{2\tau}\, e^{3(t - \tau)}\, d\tau
                                       = e^{3t}\int_0^t e^{-\tau}\, d\tau</mrow>
                      <mrow>&amp;= e^{3t}\bigl(1 - e^{-t}\bigr)
                             = e^{3t} - e^{2t}.</mrow>
                    </md>
                  Therefore <m>(f * g)(t) = e^{3t} - e^{2t}</m>.
                  </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>            
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>f(t) = e^{3t}</m> and <m>g(t) = e^{2t}</m></p>
                </statement>
                  <solution component="instructors">
                      <p>By definition,
                        <md>
                          <mrow>(f * g)(t) &amp;= \int_0^t e^{3\tau}\, e^{2(t - \tau)}\, d\tau
                                           = e^{2t}\int_0^t e^{\tau}\, d\tau</mrow>
                          <mrow>&amp;= e^{2t}\bigl(e^{t} - 1\bigr)
                                 = e^{3t} - e^{2t}.</mrow>
                        </md>
                      Therefore <m>(f * g)(t) = e^{3t} - e^{2t}</m>, in agreement with
                      the commutativity <m>f * g = g * f</m>.
                      </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

        <exercisegroup xml:id="laplace04-exercises-inverse">
            <title>Convolution and the Inverse Transform</title>
            <introduction><p>Calculate the inverse Laplace transform of <m>F(s)</m> in <xref ref="laplace04-exercises-inverse"/> using the convolution product.</p></introduction>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>F(s) = \dfrac{1}{(s - 1)(s - 3)}</m></p>
                </statement>
                 <solution component="instructors">
                    <p>
                            Write
                            <md>
                            F(s)=\frac1{s-1}\cdot\frac1{s-3}.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Then
                            <md>
                            \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1{s-1}\right)=e^{t},
                            \quad
                            \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1{s-3}\right)=e^{3t}.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Hence,
                            <md>
                            f(t)=e^{t}*e^{3t}
                            =\int_0^t e^{\tau}e^{3(t-\tau)}\,d\tau.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Evaluating,
                            <md>
                            f(t)=\frac12(e^{3t}-e^{t}).
                            </md>
                        </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>F(s) = \dfrac{3}{s(s - 1)}</m></p>
                </statement>
                 <solution component="instructors">
                    <p>
                        Write
                        <md>
                        F(s)=3\cdot\frac1s\cdot\frac1{s-1}.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then
                        <md>
                        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1s\right)=1,
                        \quad
                        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1{s-1}\right)=e^{t}.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Thus,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=3(1*e^{t})
                        =3\int_0^t e^{t-\tau}\,d\tau.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Evaluating,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=3(e^{t}-1).
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>F(s) = \dfrac{2}{s^2(s^2 + 2)}</m></p>
                </statement>
                 <solution component="instructors">
                    <p>
                        Write
                        <md>
                        F(s)=2\cdot\frac1{s^2}\cdot\frac1{s^2+2}.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then
                        <md>
                        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1{s^2}\right)=t,
                        \quad
                        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left(\frac1{s^2+2}\right)
                        =\frac1{\sqrt2}\sin(\sqrt2 t).
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=2(t*\frac1{\sqrt2}\sin(\sqrt2 t)).
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        So,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=\sqrt2\int_0^t \tau\sin(\sqrt2(t-\tau))\,d\tau.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>F(s) = \dfrac{1}{s^2 + 4s - 12}</m></p>
                </statement>
                 <solution component="instructors">
                    <p>
                        Factor,
                        <md>
                        s^2+4s-12=(s-2)(s+6).
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then
                        <md>
                        F(s)=\frac1{s-2}\cdot\frac1{s+6}.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=e^{2t}*e^{-6t}
                        =\int_0^t e^{2\tau}e^{-6(t-\tau)}\,d\tau.
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Evaluating,
                        <md>
                        f(t)=\frac18(e^{2t}-e^{-6t}).
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

        </exercisegroup>

        <exercisegroup xml:id="laplace04-exercises-solve-ivps">
            <title>Solving Initial Value Problems</title>
            <introduction><p>Solve the initial problems in <xref ref="laplace04-exercises-solve-ivps"/> using the method described in <xref ref="laplace04-subsection-applying-convolution"/>.</p></introduction>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>2 y'' + y' + 2y = g(t)</m>, <m>y(0) = 0</m>, <m>y'(0) = 0</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>Y(s) = \mathcal{L}\{y\}(s)</m> and <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>.
  Taking Laplace transforms and using <m>y(0) = y'(0) = 0</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>2s^2 Y(s) + sY(s) + 2Y(s) = G(s)
           \implies Y(s) = \frac{1}{2s^2 + s + 2}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  Completing the square,
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{1}{2s^2 + s + 2}
          = \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{\left(s + \tfrac{1}{4}\right)^2
            + \tfrac{15}{16}}
          = \frac{2}{\sqrt{15}}\cdot
            \frac{\tfrac{\sqrt{15}}{4}}{\left(s + \tfrac{1}{4}\right)^2
            + \left(\tfrac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\right)^2}.</mrow>
    </md>
  The inverse Laplace transform of this factor is
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = \frac{2}{\sqrt{15}}\, e^{-t/4}
                   \sin\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\,t\right).</mrow>
    </md>
  Since <m>Y(s) = H(s)G(s)</m>, the convolution theorem gives
    <md>
      <mrow>y(t) = (h * g)(t)
                 = \frac{2}{\sqrt{15}}\int_0^t
                   e^{-(t-\tau)/4}
                   \sin\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}(t-\tau)\right)
                   g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, <m>h(0) = 0</m> and <m>h'(0) = \tfrac{1}{2}</m>, which is
  the response of <m>2y'' + y' + 2y = \delta(t)</m> from rest.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>y'' - y' - 2y = g(t)</m>, <m>y(0) = 0</m>, <m>y'(0) = 0</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>Y(s) = \mathcal{L}\{y\}(s)</m> and
  <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>. Taking Laplace transforms and
  using <m>y(0) = y'(0) = 0</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>s^2 Y(s) - sY(s) - 2Y(s) = G(s)
           \implies Y(s) = \frac{1}{(s-2)(s+1)}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  By partial fractions,
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{1}{(s-2)(s+1)}
          = \frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{1}{s-2} - \frac{1}{s+1}\right),</mrow>
    </md>
  whose inverse Laplace transform is
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = \frac{e^{2t} - e^{-t}}{3}.</mrow>
    </md>
  Since <m>Y(s) = H(s)G(s)</m>, the convolution theorem gives
    <md>
      <mrow>y(t) = (h * g)(t)
                 = \frac{1}{3}\int_0^t
                   \left(e^{2(t-\tau)} - e^{-(t-\tau)}\right)
                   g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, <m>h(0) = 0</m> and <m>h'(0) = 1</m>, which is the
  response of <m>y'' - y' - 2y = \delta(t)</m> from rest.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>\dfrac{d^2x}{dx^2} - 6 \dfrac{dx}{dt} + 25 x = g(t)</m>, <m>x(0) = 1</m>, <m>x'(0) = -2</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>X(s) = \mathcal{L}\{x\}(s)</m> and
  <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>. Taking Laplace transforms and using
  <m>x(0) = 1</m> and <m>x'(0) = -2</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>\bigl(s^2 X - s + 2\bigr) - 6\bigl(sX - 1\bigr) + 25X = G(s),</mrow>
      <mrow>(s^2 - 6s + 25)X = G(s) + s - 8.</mrow>
    </md>
  Completing the square, <m>s^2 - 6s + 25 = (s-3)^2 + 16</m>, so
    <md>
      <mrow>X(s) = \frac{s - 8}{(s-3)^2 + 16}
                 + \frac{1}{(s-3)^2 + 16}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  For the first term, write <m>s - 8 = (s - 3) - 5</m>:
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{s-8}{(s-3)^2+16}
          = \frac{s-3}{(s-3)^2+16} - \frac{5}{4}\cdot\frac{4}{(s-3)^2+16},</mrow>
    </md>
  whose inverse transform is
  <m>e^{3t}\left(\cos 4t - \tfrac{5}{4}\sin 4t\right)</m>.
  The second factor has inverse transform
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = \frac{1}{4}\, e^{3t}\sin 4t.</mrow>
    </md>
  By the convolution theorem,
    <md>
      <mrow>x(t) = e^{3t}\left(\cos 4t - \frac{5}{4}\sin 4t\right)
                 + \frac{1}{4}\int_0^t e^{3(t-\tau)}
                   \sin\bigl(4(t-\tau)\bigr)\, g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, the first term equals <m>1</m> at <m>t = 0</m> and its
  derivative there is <m>3 - 5 = -2</m>, matching the initial conditions.
  The convolution term vanishes with its derivative at <m>t = 0</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>y'' + 16y = g(t)</m>, <m>y(0) = 1</m>, <m>y'(0) = 0</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>Y(s) = \mathcal{L}\{y\}(s)</m> and
  <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>. Taking Laplace transforms and using
  <m>y(0) = 1</m> and <m>y'(0) = 0</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>\bigl(s^2 Y - s\bigr) + 16Y = G(s)
           \implies Y(s) = \frac{s}{s^2 + 16}
           + \frac{1}{s^2 + 16}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  The inverse transform of the first term is <m>\cos 4t</m>. The second
  factor can be written as
  <m>\frac{1}{4}\cdot\frac{4}{s^2+16}</m>, with inverse transform
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = \frac{1}{4}\sin 4t.</mrow>
    </md>
  By the convolution theorem,
    <md>
      <mrow>y(t) = \cos 4t
                 + \frac{1}{4}\int_0^t \sin\bigl(4(t-\tau)\bigr)\,
                   g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, <m>\cos 4t</m> equals <m>1</m> at <m>t = 0</m> with zero
  derivative, and the convolution term vanishes together with its
  derivative at <m>t = 0</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>y'' + 16y = g(t)</m>, <m>y(0) = 1</m>, <m>y'(0) = 0</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>Y(s) = \mathcal{L}\{y\}(s)</m> and
  <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>. Taking Laplace transforms and using
  <m>y(0) = 1</m> and <m>y'(0) = 0</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>\bigl(s^2 Y - s\bigr) + 16Y = G(s)
           \implies Y(s) = \frac{s}{s^2 + 16}
           + \frac{1}{s^2 + 16}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  The inverse transform of the first term is <m>\cos 4t</m>. The second
  factor can be written as
  <m>\frac{1}{4}\cdot\frac{4}{s^2+16}</m>, with inverse transform
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = \frac{1}{4}\sin 4t.</mrow>
    </md>
  By the convolution theorem,
    <md>
      <mrow>y(t) = \cos 4t
                 + \frac{1}{4}\int_0^t \sin\bigl(4(t-\tau)\bigr)\,
                   g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, <m>\cos 4t</m> equals <m>1</m> at <m>t = 0</m> with zero
  derivative, and the convolution term vanishes together with its
  derivative at <m>t = 0</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>y'' + 2y' + y = g(t)</m>, <m>y(0) = -1</m>, <m>y'(0) = 3</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
  <p>Let <m>Y(s) = \mathcal{L}\{y\}(s)</m> and
  <m>G(s) = \mathcal{L}\{g\}(s)</m>. Taking Laplace transforms and using
  <m>y(0) = -1</m> and <m>y'(0) = 3</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>\bigl(s^2 Y + s - 3\bigr) + 2\bigl(sY + 1\bigr) + Y = G(s),</mrow>
      <mrow>(s+1)^2 Y(s) = G(s) - s + 1.</mrow>
    </md>
  Therefore
    <md>
      <mrow>Y(s) = \frac{1 - s}{(s+1)^2} + \frac{1}{(s+1)^2}\, G(s).</mrow>
    </md>
  Writing <m>1 - s = 2 - (s+1)</m>,
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{1-s}{(s+1)^2} = \frac{2}{(s+1)^2} - \frac{1}{s+1},</mrow>
    </md>
  whose inverse transform is <m>(2t - 1)e^{-t}</m>. The second factor
  has inverse transform
    <md>
      <mrow>h(t) = t e^{-t}.</mrow>
    </md>
  By the convolution theorem,
    <md>
      <mrow>y(t) = (2t - 1)e^{-t}
                 + \int_0^t (t - \tau)\, e^{-(t-\tau)}\, g(\tau)\, d\tau.</mrow>
    </md>
  As a check, <m>(2t-1)e^{-t}</m> equals <m>-1</m> at <m>t = 0</m> and
  its derivative there is <m>2 + 1 = 3</m>. The convolution term
  vanishes together with its derivative at <m>t = 0</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>
       <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Prove that the commutative property holds for the convolution product. That is, show that <m>f*g = g*f</m>.</p>
            </statement>
              <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
                By definition, the convolution of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> is
                <md>
                (f*g)(t)=\int_0^t f(\tau)g(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Make the change of variables
                <md>
                u=t-\tau, \quad d u=-d\tau.
                </md>
                When <m>\tau=0</m>, <m>u=t</m>, and when <m>\tau=t</m>, <m>u=0</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Then
                <md>
                (f*g)(t)
                =\int_t^0 f(t-u)g(u)(-du)
                =\int_0^t g(u)f(t-u)\,du.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Renaming the variable,
                <md>
                (f*g)(t)=\int_0^t g(\tau)f(t-\tau)\,d\tau
                =(g*f)(t).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore,
                <md>
                f*g=g*f.
                </md>
            </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Prove that the convolution product of two functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> distributes multiplication over addition. That is, show that <m>f*(g + h) = f*g + f*h</m> for functions <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, and <m>h</m>.</p>
            </statement>
              <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
                By definition of convolution,
                <md>
                (f*(g+h))(t)
                =\int_0^t f(\tau)(g+h)(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Using the distributive property inside the integral,
                <md>
                (f*(g+h))(t)
                =\int_0^t f(\tau)\bigl[g(t-\tau)+h(t-\tau)\bigr]\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Splitting the integral,
                <md>
                (f*(g+h))(t)
                =\int_0^t f(\tau)g(t-\tau)\,d\tau
                +\int_0^t f(\tau)h(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                By definition of convolution,
                <md>
                (f*(g+h))(t)=(f*g)(t)+(f*h)(t).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore,
                <md>
                f*(g+h)=f*g+f*h.
                </md>
            </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>     

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Prove that the convolution product is associative. In other words, show that <m>f*(g*h) = (f*g)*h</m> for functions <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, and <m>h</m>.</p>
            </statement>
              <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
                By definition of convolution,
                <md>
                (f*(g*h))(t)
                =\int_0^t f(\tau)(g*h)(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Using the definition of <m>g*h</m>,
                <md>
                (f*(g*h))(t)
                =\int_0^t f(\tau)
                \left(\int_0^{t-\tau} g(u)h(t-\tau-u)\,du\right)d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                By Fubini’s Theorem, we may change the order of integration:
                <md>
                (f*(g*h))(t)
                =\int_0^t\int_0^{t-\tau}
                f(\tau)g(u)h(t-\tau-u)\,du\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Interchanging the order,
                <md>
                (f*(g*h))(t)
                =\int_0^t\int_0^{t-u}
                f(\tau)g(u)h(t-u-\tau)\,d\tau\,du.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Using the definition of convolution again,
                <md>
                \int_0^{t-u} f(\tau)h(t-u-\tau)\,d\tau
                =(f*h)(t-u).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore,
                <md>
                (f*(g*h))(t)
                =\int_0^t g(u)(f*h)(t-u)\,du
                =( (f*h)*g )(t).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                By commutativity of convolution,
                <md>
                ( (f*h)*g )(t)=( (f*g)*h )(t).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Hence,
                <md>
                f*(g*h)=(f*g)*h.
                </md>
            </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Prove that <m>0 *f = f*0 = 0</m>.</p>
            </statement>
              <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
                Let <m>0(t)=0</m> for all <m>t\ge0</m>. By definition of convolution,
                <md>
                (0*f)(t)=\int_0^t 0(\tau)f(t-\tau)\,d\tau.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Since <m>0(\tau)=0</m> for all <m>\tau</m>,
                <md>
                (0*f)(t)=\int_0^t 0\,d\tau=0.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Similarly,
                <md>
                (f*0)(t)=\int_0^t f(\tau)0(t-\tau)\,d\tau=0.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore,
                <md>
                0*f=f*0=0.
                </md>
            </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

 </section>

